module

public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.ToLin
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.RowCol
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Span.Defs

/-!
# Generator matrices: adding one column to another preserves the code

Following Nielsen & Chuang, *Quantum Computation and Quantum Information*, §10.4.1, a **generator
matrix** `G` (an `m × n` matrix over a ring) encodes a message column vector `x` as `G *ᵥ x`, and
the **code** it generates is the set of all codewords `{G *ᵥ x}` — equivalently, the span of the
columns of `G`, i.e. the range of the encoding map `x ↦ G *ᵥ x` (`Matrix.mulVecLin`, cf.
`Matrix.range_mulVecLin`). This is the convention used throughout the classical-coding development
(`LinearCode`, `RepetitionCode`).

This file proves **Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.15** (p. 446): *adding one column of `G` to another
results in a generator matrix generating the same code.*

## Main definitions

* `Matrix.addCol M i j`: the elementary column operation that **adds column `i` to column `j`**,
  replacing column `j` of `M` with `col j + col i` and leaving every other column unchanged. This is
  right multiplication by the transvection `Matrix.transvection i j 1` (cf.
  `Matrix.mul_transvection_apply_same`).

## Main results

* `Matrix.range_mulVecLin_addCol`: for distinct columns `i ≠ j`, the code generated by
  `M.addCol i j` equals the code generated by `M`
  (`(M.addCol i j).mulVecLin.range = M.mulVecLin.range`) — Exercise 10.15.

## Implementation notes

The proof works at the level of the *columns*: the code is the span of the columns
(`Matrix.range_mulVecLin`), and `addCol` replaces one column of the family by that column plus
another (`Matrix.addCol_col`). Adding one member of a spanning family to another leaves the span
unchanged — this is the elementary-operation lemma `Submodule.span_range_update_add_smul` (applied
with scalar `1`, `v j + v i = v j + 1 • v i`). The distinctness hypothesis `i ≠ j` is
exactly what that lemma requires (adding a column *to itself* would double it, changing the family
though not its span, and is not the intended operation).

The coefficients range over an arbitrary commutative ring `R`, so the result covers N&C's binary
codes (`R = ZMod 2`) and, as N&C themselves note in Exercise 10.19, codes over more general fields.
When `R` is a field the two sides are equal linear codes in the sense of `LinearCode`.
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace Matrix

variable {α : Type*} {m n : Type*}

/-- **Add column `i` to column `j`** of the matrix `M` (Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.15): the
elementary column operation replacing column `j` with `col j + col i` and leaving all other columns
unchanged. Equivalently, right multiplication by the transvection `Matrix.transvection i j 1`. -/
def addCol [Add α] [DecidableEq n] (M : Matrix m n α) (i j : n) : Matrix m n α :=
  M.updateCol j (fun a => M a j + M a i)

/-- The entries of `M.addCol i j`: column `j` becomes `col j + col i`, every other column is
unchanged. -/
@[simp] theorem addCol_apply [Add α] [DecidableEq n] (M : Matrix m n α) (i j : n) (a : m) (c : n) :
    M.addCol i j a c = if c = j then M a j + M a i else M a c := by
  simp only [addCol, updateCol_apply]

/-- The columns of `M.addCol i j` as a family: it is `M.col` with the `j`-th entry replaced by
`M.col j + M.col i`. This is the vector-family form of `addCol`, used to reduce the code equality to
the span-level elementary-operation lemma `Submodule.span_range_update_add_smul`. -/
theorem addCol_col [Add α] [DecidableEq n] (M : Matrix m n α) (i j : n) :
    (M.addCol i j).col = Function.update M.col j (M.col j + M.col i) := by
  funext c a
  simp [col_apply, addCol_apply, Function.update_apply, ite_apply]

/-- **Adding one column of a generator matrix to another generates the same code**
(Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.15, p. 446). For distinct columns `i ≠ j`, the code generated by
`M.addCol i j` — the span of its columns, i.e. the range of the encoding map
`x ↦ (M.addCol i j) *ᵥ x` — coincides with the code generated by `M`.

The proof reduces, via `Matrix.range_mulVecLin` and `Matrix.addCol_col`, to the fact that adding
one vector of a family to another preserves the span (`Submodule.span_range_update_add_smul`, with
scalar `1`). -/
theorem range_mulVecLin_addCol {R : Type*} [CommRing R] {m n : Type*} [Fintype n] [DecidableEq n]
    (M : Matrix m n R) {i j : n} (hij : i ≠ j) :
    LinearMap.range (M.addCol i j).mulVecLin = LinearMap.range M.mulVecLin := by
  rw [range_mulVecLin, range_mulVecLin, addCol_col, ← one_smul R (M.col i)]
  exact Submodule.span_range_update_add_smul hij M.col 1

end Matrix
